# =============================================================================
# Line-ending policy for snackstore-cloud
# =============================================================================
# Policy: LF in the repository AND LF in the working tree (confirmed 2026-10).
#
# Before this file existed every path reported `text: unspecified` and
# `eol: unspecified` to `git check-attr`, i.e. conversion was delegated
# entirely to core.autocrlf. On this project's Windows development machines
# that value is not even a developer choice: it comes from Git for Windows'
# *system* gitconfig (D:/Programs/Git/etc/gitconfig -> core.autocrlf=true).
# Consequence: the same commit checked out on a Windows box yields CRLF
# working copies while a Linux/CI box with core.autocrlf=false or input
# yields LF, and whether a newly written file lands in the index as LF or
# as CRLF depends on the machine that staged it. "Clean vs dirty" was
# therefore not reliably comparable between machines.
#
# .gitattributes has higher precedence than core.autocrlf and core.eol, so
# these rules hold on every machine regardless of local or global config
# (verified: check-attr returns text=set / eol=lf under autocrlf=true,
# autocrlf=false and autocrlf=input alike).
#
# Scope note: this file governs future checkouts, adds and renormalization.
# It does NOT rewrite content that is already committed. The 9 files that
# still carry CRLF in their working copy stay byte-for-byte unchanged; they
# normalize naturally when edited or re-checked-out. Until then `git add`
# prints the informational advisory
#   warning: in the working copy of '<file>', CRLF will be replaced by LF
#   the next time Git touches it
# for those files (git diff prints nothing). A one-pass normalization is
# deliberately NOT done here; if it is ever wanted, run
#   git add --renormalize .
# and then re-check-out, in a dedicated commit containing nothing else.
#
# See docs/行尾规范说明.md for the full rationale and validation record.
# =============================================================================

# Treat every text file as LF on both sides. Unknown file types are detected
# by content (NUL/binary heuristic), so binaries are never mangled.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Source, configuration and documentation files used by this project, spelled
# out so the intent survives even if the catch-all above is ever loosened.
*.java       text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.xml        text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.sql        text eol=lf
*.txt        text eol=lf
*.csv        text eol=lf
*.dot        text eol=lf
*.dsl        text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
.gitignore   text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF to be executable inside the service containers.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch scripts are the deliberate exception: cmd.exe needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never run conversion or diff on binaries.
*.jar   binary
*.war   binary
*.class binary
*.png   binary
*.gif   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git LFS: intentionally not configured (see docs/行尾规范说明.md)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# git-lfs hooks are present in the developer's *global* config
# (filter.lfs.* with filter.lfs.required=true, installed by `git lfs install`),
# but `git lfs ls-files` returns zero objects and this repository tracks no
# binary asset at all, so an LFS filter is NOT required here. Declaring
# `filter=lfs` without a matching tracked pattern would only add smudge/clean
# overhead to every file operation.
# If a large binary is introduced later, declare that pattern explicitly, e.g.
#   *.pak filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
